::ENG2_U06_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which question helps a speaker identify purpose?{
~A. How tall is the room?#Incorrect. This does not identify the reason for speaking.
=B. Why am I speaking?#Correct. Purpose is the reason for speaking.
~C. What color is the paper?#Incorrect. This does not help with purpose.
~D. How many slides are allowed?#Incorrect. That may be a requirement, but it is not purpose.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which question helps a speaker identify audience?{
~A. What is my favorite topic?#Incorrect. This focuses on the speaker, not the listeners.
~B. How long is the hallway?#Incorrect. This is unrelated to audience.
~C. What font should I use?#Incorrect. This does not identify the listeners.
=D. Who will listen to this message?#Correct. Audience means the listeners.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which statement best shows perspective?{
=A. From a student perspective, a quiet study space would make waiting after school more productive.#Correct. This names the point of view and position.
~B. The meeting starts at 4:00.#Incorrect. This is a fact about time, not a perspective.
~C. The room has chairs.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not a point of view.
~D. The hallway is on the first floor.#Incorrect. This is location information, not perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase best fits an academic oral explanation?{
~A. a cool thing#Incorrect. This is informal.
~B. stuff after school#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. supervised academic support#Correct. This phrase is precise and academic.
~D. that place or whatever#Incorrect. This is casual and unclear.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which speaking note is a complete sentence?{
=A. Students who wait after school need a supervised place to study.#Correct. This is complete and clear.
~B. Because students wait after school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. A quiet place for students.#Incorrect. This is a phrase, not a complete sentence.
~D. Students waiting, hallway loud.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Audience: classmates. Which detail would likely connect best with this audience?{
~A. The budget code for room scheduling.#Incorrect. This is more likely useful for administrators.
~B. A full legal policy paragraph.#Incorrect. That may be too formal and detailed for classmates.
~C. The exact maintenance cost of every room.#Incorrect. This is not the most relevant student-facing detail.
=D. Students often need a quiet place to finish homework while waiting for rides.#Correct. This connects directly to student experience.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Audience: school leaders. Which tone is most appropriate?{
~A. Angry and insulting.#Incorrect. This tone would weaken communication.
=B. Respectful and specific.#Correct. School leaders need clear, respectful, specific communication.
~C. Silly and careless.#Incorrect. This does not fit a formal school audience.
~D. Confused and random.#Incorrect. This would make the message hard to follow.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] What is the best paraphrase of this idea: "A strong speaker considers what the audience needs to know"?{
~A. A speaker should ignore listeners.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. A speaker should only memorize words.#Incorrect. This misses the focus on audience.
=C. A speaker should plan information based on the listeners' needs.#Correct. This accurately restates the idea.
~D. A speaker should avoid explaining ideas.#Incorrect. This contradicts the idea.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which opening best states a clear purpose?{
~A. So, yeah, I am talking.#Incorrect. This does not state a clear purpose.
~B. I have some random things to say.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. The room is blue and the chairs are here.#Incorrect. This does not state the presentation purpose.
=D. Today I will explain why students need a supervised study space after school.#Correct. This directly states the speaking purpose.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word is most precise for a formal school audience?{
=A. perspective#Correct. Perspective is academic vocabulary that fits the lesson.
~B. stuff#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. cool#Incorrect. This is informal.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?{
~A. Students wait after school, they need a quiet space.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. Students wait after school they need a quiet space.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=C. Students wait after school, so they need a quiet space.#Correct. The conjunction so correctly joins the ideas.
~D. Because students wait after school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaking note is too informal for school leaders?{
~A. Students need a supervised academic space.#Incorrect. This is formal and appropriate.
=B. The hallway is annoying and you should fix it.#Correct. This note is too casual and demanding for school leaders.
~C. Extended library hours could support students.#Incorrect. This is appropriate.
~D. A quiet setting would help students complete assignments.#Incorrect. This is appropriate.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What should a speaker do first when planning an oral explanation?{
=A. Identify purpose, audience, and perspective.#Correct. These choices guide the entire oral message.
~B. Choose random words to sound impressive.#Incorrect. Word choice should serve the audience and purpose.
~C. Ignore the assigned task.#Incorrect. The task defines the purpose.
~D. Write a closing before knowing the topic.#Incorrect. Planning should begin with the communication situation.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Passage idea: Students can help adults understand daily school life. What does this mean?{
~A. Adults never understand anything.#Incorrect. This overstates the idea.
~B. Students should avoid speaking at meetings.#Incorrect. The passage supports student communication.
~C. Daily school life is never important.#Incorrect. The passage says it matters.
=D. Students can share firsthand school experiences that adults may not see every day.#Correct. This accurately explains the passage idea.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which statement best matches a student perspective?{
~A. The building was constructed many years ago.#Incorrect. This is not a student perspective about the issue.
~B. The budget has many categories.#Incorrect. This is more administrative.
=C. Students who remain after school often need a quiet place to work.#Correct. This presents a student-centered perspective.
~D. Room numbers are listed on the map.#Incorrect. This is a detail, not a perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses standard English conventions?{
~A. Students needs support after school.#Incorrect. Students needs should be students need.
=B. Students need support after school because the hallways are loud.#Correct. This sentence is complete and clear.
~C. Because the hallways loud.#Incorrect. This is a fragment and missing a verb.
~D. Students wait after school, need quiet.#Incorrect. This is not a complete clear sentence.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which revision best adapts the message for school leaders?{
~A. Give us a room because hallways are bad.#Incorrect. This is too informal and demanding.
~B. I hate waiting after school.#Incorrect. This is personal and not developed for the audience.
=C. School leaders should consider a supervised study space because it would support students who remain on campus after dismissal.#Correct. This is respectful, specific, and audience-aware.
~D. Everybody knows this is a problem.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase best replaces "kids need a place"?{
=A. students need a supervised workspace#Correct. This phrase is precise and academic.
~B. kids need stuff#Incorrect. This remains informal and vague.
~C. people need somewhere nice#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. everybody needs things#Incorrect. This is too vague.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker choice best supports listener understanding?{
~A. Jump between unrelated ideas.#Incorrect. This makes the message confusing.
=B. Organize ideas in a clear order.#Correct. Clear organization helps listeners follow the message.
~C. Use only slang.#Incorrect. Slang may not fit the audience.
~D. Avoid explaining the purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose helps listeners understand the message.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] What detail from the passage best supports respectful student communication?{
~A. "A strong speaker does not simply say, 'This is what I want.'"#Incorrect. This shows what not to do, but another detail directly names respect.
~B. "student asking for more after-school study space"#Incorrect. This gives an example topic, not the respect focus.
~C. "who needs the space"#Incorrect. This is about detail, not respect.
=D. "presents a perspective with respect"#Correct. This directly supports respectful communication.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is clearest for oral delivery?{
=A. A supervised study space would help students complete assignments after school.#Correct. The sentence is clear and easy to follow.
~B. Space study after school supervised students assignments.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~C. It is the thing that does the stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Because after school students.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which choice best explains audience awareness?{
~A. Saying whatever comes to mind.#Incorrect. Audience awareness requires planning.
~B. Speaking without a purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose is necessary.
=C. Choosing details and tone based on who is listening.#Correct. Audience awareness means adapting communication for listeners.
~D. Avoiding all specific details.#Incorrect. Specific details help the audience understand.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which closing best reinforces the speaker's purpose?{
~A. Anyway, that is all.#Incorrect. This is vague and weak.
~B. I am done now.#Incorrect. This does not reinforce the purpose.
~C. The chairs are in the room.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
=D. A supervised study space would give students a safer and more productive place to wait after school.#Correct. This reinforces the purpose clearly.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase is least appropriate for academic oral communication?{
~A. student perspective#Incorrect. This is academic and appropriate.
=B. random stuff#Correct. This is vague and informal.
~C. intended audience#Incorrect. This is academic and appropriate.
~D. communication purpose#Incorrect. This is academic and appropriate.
}



::ENG2_U06_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which checklist best matches Lesson 1?{
=A. Identify purpose, audience, perspective, details, tone, and speaking notes.#Correct. This checklist matches the lesson process.
~B. Choose slang, skip purpose, and ignore the audience.#Incorrect. This works against clear oral communication.
~C. Read unrelated facts in any order.#Incorrect. Organization and relevance matter.
~D. Focus only on volume and ignore content.#Incorrect. Clear content is required.
}
